ntdll,server: map a PE shared section on a host with larger pages than the image.

A writable shared PE section (IMAGE_SCN_MEM_SHARED | IMAGE_SCN_MEM_WRITE) must be mapped MAP_SHARED, and
mmap() can only place a shared mapping on a whole host page. On a host whose pages are LARGER than the
image's section alignment the two granularities disagree, and wine gives up on the image entirely:

    err:virtual:map_file_into_view unaligned shared mapping 0x141b55000-0x141b56000 not supported
    err:module:map_image_into_view Could not map L"C:\\game\\x64\\ShadowOfMordor.exe" shared section .SHARED
    wine: failed to start L"C:\\game\\x64\\ShadowOfMordor.exe": c000007b

That is Middle-earth: Shadow of Mordor on a 16k-page aarch64 host. Its `.SHARED` section is EIGHT BYTES
at 0x141b55000 — which is 0 mod 4096 and 4096 mod 16384 — so a 54 GB game does not start because of one
8-byte cross-instance variable. The same image loads on any 4k host, where the address is aligned by
construction. This is not a regression: before the host-page-size abstraction (2025-03-25) wine attempted
the mmap, the kernel refused with EINVAL, and `if (flags & MAP_SHARED) return STATUS_INVALID_PARAMETER;`
produced the same dead end. The abstraction only replaced the failed syscall with a predictive check, and
added the diagnostic that makes the cause legible.

Two changes, in order of preference at runtime.

1. KEEP THE SECTION SHARED WHERE THAT IS POSSIBLE. The backing store for shared sections is a temp file
   the SERVER creates and lays out itself (build_shared_mapping), so the file-offset side of the
   congruence is ours to pick. Place every shared section at an offset congruent to its virtual address
   modulo the host page size, and the client can round address and offset down together and map the
   ENCLOSING host pages. On a 4k host the congruence already holds — addresses are multiples of the
   (>= 4k) section alignment and every map_size is rounded to it — so no padding is emitted and the file
   layout is byte-for-byte what it was.

   The client only does this when the extra bytes belong to NOBODY ELSE (shared_section_is_page_isolated):
   they come from the shared file, so another section sharing the host page would have its contents
   replaced and its protection and cross-process semantics changed with them. Shadow of Mordor does not
   qualify — `.pdata` ends and an executable `text` section begins inside the same 16k page — which is
   exactly why the check is a check and not an assumption.

2. OTHERWISE MAP IT PRIVATELY, AND SAY SO. When the shared mapping cannot be placed, prefer a loadable
   image to a correct one: fall into the read() path that already serves every misaligned NON-shared
   section on such a host. The section then silently stops being shared between processes, which is the
   kind of defect that surfaces much later as inexplicable behaviour, so it is reported at ERR level with
   the range, both page sizes, and what was given up. Games that use a shared section for multi-instance
   coordination (Shadow of Mordor's 8 bytes) do not care; one that genuinely shares state across
   processes would, and the log line is how that gets diagnosed rather than guessed.

   Gated on host_page_mask > page_mask: where the two match, an unmappable shared section means the file
   or the fd is unusable and the image must still be refused.
